layout: app-layout.njk
title: Strengths / Weaknesses – Climbing Logbook
discipline: true
bundle: performance-strengths
staticShellNote: |-
  Genuinely static shell (#348), same pattern as public/map/index.html
         -- see that file's own comment for why every path below is absolute
         and client/performance-strengths-main.js reads the username from
         location.pathname itself rather than this file being templated.
---
{% from "tab-bar.njk" import tabBar %}
<div class="max-w-[960px] mx-auto">
  <div class="flex items-end gap-3 mb-2" id="picker-tabs-row">
    <climbing-discipline-picker></climbing-discipline-picker>
    {% call tabBar("performance") %}{% include "tabs-log.njk" %}{% endcall %}
  </div>

  <a class="back-link mt-4 mb-5" id="back-to-performance-link" href="#">
    <svg class="w-[.9rem] h-[.9rem] stroke-current fill-none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m12 19-7-7 7-7"></path><path d="M19 12H5"></path></svg>
    Performance Insights
  </a>

  <h1 class="page-title">Strengths / Weaknesses</h1>

  <div class="mb-5" id="view-explainer">
    <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-[1.7]">Where you're strong and weak: by wall angle, and by the holds, moves and positions that make climbs hard for you.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-[1.7] mt-2">Built from the <strong class="text-foreground font-semibold">Wall angle</strong> and <strong class="text-foreground font-semibold">Hardest move/sequence</strong> you add on each climb, except Checkouts. Something only counts as a weakness when it's hard on climbs below your level, so climbing a lot of it won't make it look weak.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="row-card text-center max-w-[420px] mx-auto" id="performance-offline" hidden>
    <p class="text-sm text-foreground">Performance insights need a connection.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">Reconnect and reload this page to see your strengths and weaknesses.</p>
  </div>

  <div id="strengths-root">{% loadingSpinner "Loading your Strengths / Weaknesses" %}</div>

  <h2 class="sources-heading">Sources</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-[1.7] mb-3">Breaking performance down by hold type, wall angle, and movement style rather than grade alone follows two converging lines of evidence: finger/grip strength is the strongest measurable performance determinant in the climbing-science literature, and unaddressed technique gaps -- not just physical capacity -- are a well-documented cause of plateaus.</p>
  <ol class="m-0 pl-[1.2rem] text-sm leading-[1.6] text-foreground [&>li+li]:mt-[10px]">
    <li>MacLeod, D. (2010). <em class="text-muted italic">9 Out of 10 Climbers Make the Same Mistakes: Navigation Through the Maze of Advice for the Self-coached Climber</em>. The book's central thesis -- that most climbers plateau from unaddressed technical/tactical errors, not lack of physical training -- is the reason this view segments by terrain and movement, not just raw send counts.</li>
    <li>Buraas, B. F., Brobakken, M. F., &amp; Wang, E. (2025). "Climbing performance in males: the importance of climbing-specific finger strength." <em class="text-muted italic">European Journal of Applied Physiology</em>, 125(10), 2823–2830. DOI: <a class="text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-2" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00421-025-05802-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">10.1007/s00421-025-05802-5 ↗</a></li>
    <li>Faggian, S., et al. (2024). "Sport climbing performance determinants and functional testing methods: A systematic review." <em class="text-muted italic">Journal of Sport and Health Science</em>, 14. <a class="text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-2" href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11904605/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ↗</a></li>
    <li>Vigouroux, L., Domalain, M., &amp; Berton, E. (2011). "Effect of Object Width on Muscle and Joint Forces During Thumb–Index Finger Grasping." <em class="text-muted italic">Journal of Applied Biomechanics</em>, 27(3), 173–180. Biomechanical basis for why grip/hold width changes joint loading -- the reason hold type is tracked as its own dimension, not folded into a generic difficulty score.</li>
  </ol>

</div>
